00:00:27.915 --> 00:00:32.835
There are many, I would say,
related languages in the world, uh,

00:00:33.625 --> 00:00:35.145
geographically close to each other.

00:00:35.415 --> 00:00:39.735
Um, vocabulary syntax and so forth
that are close to each other.

00:00:40.095 --> 00:00:44.175
Uh, and, uh, but sometimes this
makes it more difficult to learn the

00:00:44.180 --> 00:00:45.525
language because it's so similar.

00:00:46.035 --> 00:00:47.535
And so I want to talk today.

00:00:48.589 --> 00:00:52.379
Because Ksenia is in the position of
a native speaker of Russian, in fact,

00:00:52.379 --> 00:00:57.510
someone from Russia who lived in Ukraine
and learned Ukrainian to, I would say

00:00:57.510 --> 00:01:01.815
native, to a native level, but it's
something that's of interest to me.

00:01:01.815 --> 00:01:06.225
A because of my interest in Ukraine
and Russia, but also because it comes

00:01:06.225 --> 00:01:10.935
up when, say Spanish speakers try
to learn Portuguese or, uh, people

00:01:10.935 --> 00:01:15.435
from uh, Spain wanna learn Catalan
or Swedes have to learn Danish.

00:01:15.945 --> 00:01:18.765
Uh, there are advantages
and disadvantages.

00:01:19.125 --> 00:01:21.310
So my first question is how...

00:01:21.690 --> 00:01:22.610
all right.

00:01:23.205 --> 00:01:25.335
Question number one, a Russian sp...

00:01:25.335 --> 00:01:28.845
someone from Russia who has
never heard Ukrainian in his

00:01:28.845 --> 00:01:30.824
or her life hears Ukrainian.

00:01:31.185 --> 00:01:32.744
What percentage do they understand?

00:01:34.604 --> 00:01:40.494
I would say, hmm, maybe,
uh, maybe about 40 or 50.

00:01:40.925 --> 00:01:41.535
Mm-hmm.

00:01:41.804 --> 00:01:44.595
, uh, but not more,
especially when people talk.

00:01:45.360 --> 00:01:45.970
Mm-hmm.

00:01:46.054 --> 00:01:50.820
Uh, so, and also it depends on the
sphere, uh, we are talking about.

00:01:50.820 --> 00:01:55.770
So for example, right, if you go to the
restaurant and you want to order something

00:01:55.770 --> 00:01:57.600
there, you will understand, of course.

00:01:57.780 --> 00:01:57.960
Mm-hmm.

00:01:58.210 --> 00:02:01.830
But if you listen, uh, if you are
listening to the radio station,

00:02:02.160 --> 00:02:07.490
uh, I'm afraid that you probably
will understand like 30% if you

00:02:07.490 --> 00:02:11.620
have never, um, if you have never
heard Ukrainian, because they sp...

00:02:11.620 --> 00:02:16.580
uh, they speak very fast and they
use a lot of, uh, words that are

00:02:16.585 --> 00:02:18.890
different from, uh, Russian vocabulary.

00:02:19.220 --> 00:02:23.300
Uh, that's actually what I faced
with when I moved to Ukraine.

00:02:23.340 --> 00:02:23.740
Mm-hmm.

00:02:23.825 --> 00:02:25.220
Because I don't have Ukrainian.

00:02:25.625 --> 00:02:26.235
Mm-hmm.

00:02:26.315 --> 00:02:29.035
I've never been in the
Ukrainian environment.

00:02:29.275 --> 00:02:33.655
Uh, I didn't travel to Ukraine
maybe once or twice, just for

00:02:33.660 --> 00:02:36.745
several days, and I moved for work.

00:02:36.985 --> 00:02:42.325
Uh, although I was not required to learn
Ukrainian because our radio station was

00:02:42.330 --> 00:02:47.245
half Russian and half Ukrainian, and I
was allowed to use, uh, Russian language.

00:02:47.575 --> 00:02:47.665
Mm-hmm.

00:02:48.155 --> 00:02:51.055
, I decided from the first day
that I would learn Ukrainian.

00:02:51.450 --> 00:02:55.980
, uh, first out of respect because I
think it's a, it's an essential thing

00:02:55.980 --> 00:02:57.600
when you move to another country.

00:02:57.840 --> 00:02:57.930
Mm-hmm.

00:02:58.170 --> 00:03:02.100
, you need to at least to make an
effort to learn  the language

00:03:02.100 --> 00:03:03.690
of that country out of respect.

00:03:03.790 --> 00:03:04.400
Mm-hmm.

00:03:04.485 --> 00:03:09.600
. Uh, and, uh, also I just like languages
and, uh, I love learning languages.

00:03:09.600 --> 00:03:16.400
So I decided to, to start right
away, uh, and, um, , remember

00:03:16.405 --> 00:03:18.690
how I started with listening.

00:03:18.690 --> 00:03:18.780
Mm-hmm.

00:03:19.325 --> 00:03:23.010
because we are, we were preparing to
launch a new radio station mm-hmm.

00:03:23.250 --> 00:03:28.920
And to be able to talk with the
guests on radio, on air, I was,

00:03:28.920 --> 00:03:30.780
uh, I needed to understand them.

00:03:31.170 --> 00:03:33.450
Uh, and that was a
problem in the beginning.

00:03:33.455 --> 00:03:37.840
I remember I was listening to, uh,
just some interviews on the radio.

00:03:37.840 --> 00:03:42.630
I recorded them and I was sitting
with my teacher and listening to them,

00:03:42.630 --> 00:03:44.350
and I didn't understand anything.

00:03:44.350 --> 00:03:44.870
Mm-hmm.

00:03:44.955 --> 00:03:48.900
I did understand some words, but
I couldn't understand the whole

00:03:49.170 --> 00:03:53.550
sentence or I couldn't, uh, tell
what, what they were talking about.

00:03:53.670 --> 00:03:55.710
So we just stopped the recordings.

00:03:55.860 --> 00:03:55.950
Mm-hmm.

00:03:56.190 --> 00:03:58.290
And I asked her, what was that word?

00:03:58.290 --> 00:03:59.370
What was that word?

00:03:59.670 --> 00:04:02.400
And then my ear get adjusted to that.

00:04:02.640 --> 00:04:08.285
Uh, and after that, uh, I started, so
first I adjusted my ear to Ukrainian.

00:04:08.285 --> 00:04:15.065
And of course I started reading, but
uh, then I started to, uh, try speaking.

00:04:15.365 --> 00:04:17.285
Uh, and, uh, I took classes.

00:04:17.735 --> 00:04:20.825
I took classes, uh, very, very
often, uh, the first time.

00:04:21.395 --> 00:04:21.635
Right.

00:04:21.635 --> 00:04:23.945
Couple of questions because you
said some very interesting things.

00:04:24.245 --> 00:04:25.145
First of all.

00:04:25.625 --> 00:04:30.455
So 40 to 50% comprehension Russian
speakers who have never heard Ukrainian.

00:04:30.695 --> 00:04:32.105
Okay, so 40, 50%.

00:04:32.375 --> 00:04:38.505
Is that about the same as say,
Czech or, or are they even less

00:04:38.505 --> 00:04:40.185
comprehensible to Russian speakers?

00:04:40.695 --> 00:04:41.655
Uh, even less.

00:04:41.685 --> 00:04:42.165
Even less.

00:04:42.225 --> 00:04:47.265
The interesting thing is that,
for example, if you know Russian

00:04:47.325 --> 00:04:51.195
and Belarusian language, you
can understand Ukrainian.

00:04:51.385 --> 00:04:51.805
Yes.

00:04:51.810 --> 00:04:56.505
If you know Russian and Ukrainian,
you can understand Belarusian.

00:04:56.715 --> 00:05:01.815
But if you know only Russian, you can't
understand Ukrainian or Belarusian.

00:05:01.815 --> 00:05:03.505
That's a very interesting phenomenon.

00:05:03.935 --> 00:05:05.520
but Polish and Czech, even less.

00:05:05.970 --> 00:05:10.540
Polish and Czech, even less, uh, Ukrainian
people understand Polish language.

00:05:11.335 --> 00:05:11.695
Mm-hmm.

00:05:12.105 --> 00:05:15.795
. 
Second question or second point was you
made a very important point, and that

00:05:15.795 --> 00:05:20.400
is when you arrived in Ukraine, you
decided right away to learn Ukrainian.

00:05:21.090 --> 00:05:25.755
I think if you had not done so, if
you say waited a year, you would

00:05:26.215 --> 00:05:27.645
probably be less motivated to learn.

00:05:27.855 --> 00:05:31.995
I always say that when you first arrived
in the country, that's when you have

00:05:31.995 --> 00:05:36.195
to attack the language and then you
develop habits of operating in the

00:05:36.195 --> 00:05:40.544
language of the country rather than
operating in your own native language.

00:05:41.145 --> 00:05:44.294
Uh, and the third thing you said was
that comprehension was so important

00:05:44.594 --> 00:05:47.984
and, and that's why I started learning
Ukrainian, because I was watching Schuster

00:05:47.989 --> 00:05:53.534
Live on Ukrainian TV in 2014 following
the events in the Donbas and so forth.

00:05:53.655 --> 00:05:57.914
And as you, as you said, in those days,
half the people on television spoke

00:05:57.914 --> 00:06:00.135
Ukrainian, half of them spoke Russian.

00:06:00.224 --> 00:06:02.354
And I couldn't understand the Ukrainians.

00:06:02.474 --> 00:06:03.885
I mean, I can understand some of it.

00:06:04.005 --> 00:06:06.950
I felt I should be able to
understand but I couldn't.

00:06:07.369 --> 00:06:10.340
And there's also been an evolution
in Ukraine where political

00:06:10.340 --> 00:06:12.200
leaders now never use Russian.

00:06:12.380 --> 00:06:17.270
Whereas in 2014, a number of the members
of the government even spoke in Russian

00:06:17.270 --> 00:06:19.280
all the time, which is no longer the case.

00:06:19.609 --> 00:06:25.729
So to what extent though, as a speaker
of Russian and living in Ukraine,

00:06:25.729 --> 00:06:27.859
you can communicate in Russian.

00:06:27.890 --> 00:06:29.539
Everybody will understand you.

00:06:29.870 --> 00:06:33.200
Half the people, depending on where you
are in the country, are native speakers

00:06:33.200 --> 00:06:38.369
of Russian, so there would be a tremendous
temptation to just speak Russian.

00:06:38.789 --> 00:06:42.520
Was it difficult for you to force
yourself to speak Ukrainian?

00:06:43.650 --> 00:06:45.540
Well, I spoke Russian a lot.

00:06:45.540 --> 00:06:50.550
I still spoke Russian a lot, but I
tried to speak Ukrainian on radio

00:06:50.640 --> 00:06:52.710
with Ukrainian speaking guests.

00:06:53.040 --> 00:06:53.130
Mm-hmm.

00:06:53.370 --> 00:06:58.020
uh, and uh, I tried to speak Ukrainian
with my Ukrainian speaking friends.

00:06:58.025 --> 00:06:58.110
Mm-hmm.

00:06:58.620 --> 00:07:02.820
uh, because I had friends, for
example, who spoke, um, they

00:07:03.080 --> 00:07:04.500
didn't speak Russian very well.

00:07:04.500 --> 00:07:05.040
Mm-hmm.

00:07:05.159 --> 00:07:09.090
so Russian was definitely as
a foreign language for them.

00:07:09.090 --> 00:07:12.650
They were able to speak Russian,
but uh, they made an effort.

00:07:13.169 --> 00:07:13.630
Mm-hmm.

00:07:13.630 --> 00:07:17.775
uh, and I tried to speak Ukrainian,
uh, uh, Ukrainian with them.

00:07:17.924 --> 00:07:22.905
And also maybe it would be, uh,
interesting that, um, when I tried

00:07:22.905 --> 00:07:27.614
to speak Ukrainian on air, since I
was allowed to speak Russian, I did

00:07:27.614 --> 00:07:34.724
this, uh, I started, uh, implementing
Ukrainian sentences and phrases.

00:07:34.729 --> 00:07:34.865
Mm-hmm.

00:07:34.945 --> 00:07:36.265
just one by one.

00:07:36.625 --> 00:07:37.065
Mm-hmm.

00:07:37.094 --> 00:07:40.575
Uh, for, for example, uh,
I am on there and I have.

00:07:41.205 --> 00:07:44.325
Ukrainian guest on the
phone, not in the studio.

00:07:44.775 --> 00:07:44.955
Right.

00:07:44.955 --> 00:07:46.965
And I had a co-host.

00:07:47.354 --> 00:07:47.445
Mm-hmm.

00:07:47.989 --> 00:07:50.325
So we spoke Russian
with my co-host mm-hmm.

00:07:50.570 --> 00:07:55.094
And we have a Ukrainian speaking
guest, and I tried to ask, uh,

00:07:55.185 --> 00:07:59.385
them a couple of questions in
Ukrainian just for, for the start.

00:08:00.145 --> 00:08:01.885
Uh, to make it less stressful.

00:08:02.055 --> 00:08:02.665
Mm-hmm.

00:08:02.745 --> 00:08:05.665
so I would speak Russians and
I switch to Ukrainian to ask

00:08:05.725 --> 00:08:07.765
only one or two questions.

00:08:07.795 --> 00:08:07.885
Mm-hmm.

00:08:08.125 --> 00:08:13.165
Then my co-host, uh, continues,
and, uh, then I say goodbye,

00:08:13.165 --> 00:08:14.485
for example, in Ukrainian.

00:08:14.485 --> 00:08:17.125
And thank you for, uh, for being with us.

00:08:17.365 --> 00:08:19.765
Uh, then, uh, let's say in a.

00:08:20.565 --> 00:08:24.625
Uh, I will ask more like three or
five questions in the Ukrainian...

00:08:24.625 --> 00:08:30.405
to what extent you continue to mix,
to what extent almost not intending to

00:08:30.409 --> 00:08:35.414
mix the two, but you're trying to speak
Ukrainian and Russian words show up to, to

00:08:35.414 --> 00:08:37.095
what extent did that continue to happen?

00:08:38.294 --> 00:08:43.245
Um, I, I, I mixed it to the, to
some point, and, uh, I, at some.

00:08:44.145 --> 00:08:49.995
I was able to be on air, like,
let's say, um, half an hour of the

00:08:49.995 --> 00:08:52.574
whole hour speaking only Ukrainian.

00:08:52.875 --> 00:08:53.775
With no Russian words.

00:08:53.985 --> 00:08:55.185
No russian words at all.

00:08:55.185 --> 00:08:55.344
Yes.

00:08:55.344 --> 00:08:55.545
No

00:08:55.545 --> 00:08:58.964
... 
I'm sorry.

00:08:58.964 --> 00:08:59.055
No

00:08:59.550 --> 00:09:05.189
.... 
oh, no, no, no ... No ... I think
people who learn Ukrainian, they are

00:09:05.189 --> 00:09:10.439
more purist, uh, than people who are
native Ukrainians because I think it's

00:09:10.439 --> 00:09:13.949
the common thing within your language,
when you are learning the language, you

00:09:14.370 --> 00:09:19.079
are trying to speak pure language and
we are trying to avoid the mistakes.

00:09:19.079 --> 00:09:22.319
And that's why I think, uh, those who
learn Ukrainians, they try to avoid

00:09:22.640 --> 00:09:25.695
...  
a question: as a Russian speaker.

00:09:25.815 --> 00:09:27.945
What was most difficult in Ukrainian?

00:09:28.005 --> 00:09:31.065
Was it the grammar, the
vocabulary, or the pronunciation?

00:09:32.235 --> 00:09:35.385
I would say the most, uh, most
difficult thing for the Russian

00:09:35.390 --> 00:09:37.605
speakers is always the pronunciation.

00:09:37.875 --> 00:09:42.765
It's very different, uh, in Ukrainian
and the vocabulary in the second place.

00:09:43.155 --> 00:09:48.600
Um, Ukrainians are very,
Picky about pronunciation.

00:09:48.960 --> 00:09:52.830
Uh, so, you know, they're, uh,
uh, the legends or maybe it's

00:09:52.830 --> 00:09:54.780
truth about French people.

00:09:54.960 --> 00:09:57.960
I know French, but French
people never corrected me, so

00:09:57.960 --> 00:09:59.220
I didn't have that experience.

00:09:59.220 --> 00:10:02.670
But I know that, that there is a
stereotype that French people would

00:10:02.670 --> 00:10:07.740
correct you, uh, when  when you say
something not in the proper way.

00:10:08.040 --> 00:10:08.190
Mm-hmm.

00:10:08.430 --> 00:10:14.045
and Ukrainians, same, uh, they're very,
uh, very picky about pronunciation

00:10:14.045 --> 00:10:15.875
and about using the right word.

00:10:15.875 --> 00:10:19.445
So they would correct you and
sometimes they would make fun of you.

00:10:19.835 --> 00:10:25.025
Uh, and uh, that's what, um, I, I
don't like that approach very much.

00:10:25.025 --> 00:10:29.825
And I think they understood that,
uh, it was not right because when the

00:10:29.825 --> 00:10:34.895
war began, I saw, uh, you know, like
the notes in the restaurants and the

00:10:34.895 --> 00:10:41.615
coffee shops, um, uh, Russian, uh,
Speaking people who are, uh, trying

00:10:41.615 --> 00:10:44.375
to speak Ukrainian, you are not funny.

00:10:44.555 --> 00:10:45.695
Uh, you are wonderful.

00:10:45.915 --> 00:10:46.335
Yes.

00:10:46.340 --> 00:10:50.045
Uh, so they changed that
approach, uh, which is very good.

00:10:50.225 --> 00:10:51.695
But they used to be like that.

00:10:51.785 --> 00:10:56.915
So I was afraid to speak Ukrainian and
that's why I worked on my pronunciation.

00:10:57.245 --> 00:11:01.805
One more life hack: I have a son
and, uh, he was only three years

00:11:01.805 --> 00:11:03.755
old when we moved to Ukraine.

00:11:04.045 --> 00:11:04.655
Mm-hmm.

00:11:04.734 --> 00:11:09.654
So I tried to read, uh, books to
him in Ukrainian every evening.

00:11:10.185 --> 00:11:16.104
And that helped a lot because it's
a simple vocabulary, uh, in, uh,

00:11:16.104 --> 00:11:19.974
children's books and, uh, I was
practicing Ukrainian like that.

00:11:20.064 --> 00:11:22.895
And also, uh, on the radio
and during my lessons.

00:11:23.855 --> 00:11:24.095
Mm-hmm.

00:11:24.255 --> 00:11:31.155
You know, I, I always say unsolicited
criticism is absolutely not welcome.

00:11:31.395 --> 00:11:35.625
I don't think anyone should correct
another person speaking a language

00:11:35.655 --> 00:11:39.720
unless they deliberately ask for it
because all it does is discourage them.

00:11:40.140 --> 00:11:41.880
Basically, people just wanna communicate.

00:11:42.329 --> 00:11:47.850
I must say, me as a Canadian, no
Ukrainian ever criticizes me or my

00:11:47.850 --> 00:11:50.280
Ukrainian, however poorly I speak.

00:11:50.370 --> 00:11:52.350
They say, wow, your
Ukrainian is wonderful.

00:11:52.500 --> 00:11:55.980
So I think as a Russian speaker,
and they don't know maybe whether

00:11:55.980 --> 00:11:58.500
you're from Russia or you're a
Ukrainian Russian speaker, whatever,

00:11:58.650 --> 00:11:59.939
they might be more demanding.

00:12:00.210 --> 00:12:00.640
for me to...

00:12:00.665 --> 00:12:01.065
yes.

00:12:01.070 --> 00:12:01.205
Yes.

00:12:01.205 --> 00:12:02.075
I think that's important.

00:12:02.075 --> 00:12:02.795
...not demanding it all.

00:12:02.795 --> 00:12:04.705
They just, whatever I
do is good, you know?

00:12:04.710 --> 00:12:06.005
So, yeah.

00:12:06.155 --> 00:12:09.755
But I, I think in general, we
shouldn't criticize people.

00:12:09.965 --> 00:12:10.055
Mm-hmm.

00:12:10.300 --> 00:12:13.805
if they're speaking our language,
I would never, if I speak to

00:12:13.805 --> 00:12:16.415
someone in English and they make
mistakes, I would never correct them.

00:12:16.475 --> 00:12:17.105
Never correct them.

00:12:17.595 --> 00:12:18.525
Oh, I agree with you.

00:12:18.525 --> 00:12:19.075
I know, yes.

00:12:19.415 --> 00:12:22.215
And so, and the second thing
is vocabulary, as I said.

00:12:22.455 --> 00:12:26.385
Uh, yeah, because, uh, in Ukrainian
language, there are a lot of words

00:12:26.385 --> 00:12:30.495
that, um, are completely different
from, uh, the Russian ones.

00:12:30.735 --> 00:12:35.295
Uh, and, uh, I was, uh, just for fun,
I was, uh, writing some sentences

00:12:35.295 --> 00:12:39.195
that are completely different that
they, they don't have any common

00:12:39.195 --> 00:12:41.445
word in Russian and in the Ukrainian.

00:12:41.535 --> 00:12:46.195
Uh, so, uh, if you want to say,
for example, in Russian and in the

00:12:46.200 --> 00:12:51.450
Ukrainian the sentence uh, an educated
person should use, uh, an should

00:12:51.450 --> 00:12:53.820
use independent, uh, resources.

00:12:54.439 --> 00:12:55.050
Mm-hmm.

00:12:55.135 --> 00:12:57.630
In Russian, it would sound like, uh

00:13:01.650 --> 00:13:04.470
...
and in Ukrainian it will sound like

00:13:04.920 --> 00:13:16.165
...
And you can't find any word in common in
those two sentences, so it shows you that

00:13:16.255 --> 00:13:20.725
the languages are very different, that
it's not some kind of twisted Russian,

00:13:20.935 --> 00:13:22.885
uh, there is a stereotype like that.

00:13:22.915 --> 00:13:27.655
Uh, actually it's a propaganda,
propagandist myth, uh, in Russia that, uh,

00:13:27.715 --> 00:13:31.485
Russian language and Belarusian language
there some kind of twisted Russian.

00:13:32.235 --> 00:13:37.395
No, they're not, uh, in fact, Belarussian
is very similar to Ukrainian and I, I'm

00:13:37.395 --> 00:13:41.865
not, it's not clear in my mind to what
extent that's due to the original sort

00:13:41.865 --> 00:13:46.814
of church slavonic, which was the origin
of these Eastern slavic languages, or the

00:13:46.814 --> 00:13:51.689
fact that Belarus and Ukraine were part
of the Polish Lithuanian commonwealth

00:13:51.689 --> 00:13:56.730
for three, 400 years, and I dunno who
influenced who, whether the Ukrainian

00:13:57.120 --> 00:14:01.110
language influenced Polish or whether the
Polish language influenced the Ukrainian.

00:14:01.115 --> 00:14:03.689
But the vocabulary in Polish is closer.

00:14:04.290 --> 00:14:08.100
Polish and Ukrainian are closer
in terms of vocabulary than

00:14:08.105 --> 00:14:09.300
either one is to Russian.

00:14:09.300 --> 00:14:13.350
Russian is kind of the, even amongst
all the Slavic languages, Russian

00:14:13.350 --> 00:14:15.000
is a bit of an outlier, right.

00:14:15.000 --> 00:14:17.910
Even Slovak and Czech have
more vocabulary in common.

00:14:18.060 --> 00:14:21.165
I'm not sure what the historical
reasons for that,you know, are,

00:14:21.165 --> 00:14:22.454
but that is in fact the fact.

00:14:22.574 --> 00:14:23.055
Yes.

00:14:23.060 --> 00:14:26.655
So I think Ukrainian language
was, uh, definitely influenced

00:14:26.655 --> 00:14:28.094
by Polish language a lot.

00:14:28.099 --> 00:14:32.655
And Polish language, uh, was
influenced by German language.

00:14:32.660 --> 00:14:36.584
That's why in Ukrainian, actually
there are a lot of words, uh,

00:14:36.765 --> 00:14:39.015
common with, uh, with German roots.

00:14:39.135 --> 00:14:40.155
Uh, and you can recognize...

00:14:40.375 --> 00:14:43.605
in russian as well, but in Russian as
well, there's lots of German words.

00:14:44.485 --> 00:14:45.285
Uh, yes.

00:14:45.300 --> 00:14:45.690
Yes.

00:14:45.690 --> 00:14:47.070
But above they're different.

00:14:47.630 --> 00:14:48.350
They're different.

00:14:48.350 --> 00:14:49.150
And from different, and different.

00:14:49.150 --> 00:14:51.060
Different, uh, there's
always different eras.

00:14:51.720 --> 00:14:51.960
Yeah.

00:14:52.410 --> 00:14:53.250
There's always influence.

00:14:53.430 --> 00:14:57.630
But people say, well, you know, Russian is
full of Finno-Ugric words or Tatar words.

00:14:57.750 --> 00:14:58.440
That's not true.

00:14:58.445 --> 00:15:03.450
There's hardly any, there's far more
Western European words in Russian than

00:15:03.450 --> 00:15:07.305
Finno-Ugric or, or Tatar origin words.

00:15:07.305 --> 00:15:11.865
So I think all of those languages
were influenced by, you know, European

00:15:11.955 --> 00:15:14.655
culture, whatever literature words.

00:15:14.660 --> 00:15:18.255
Yes, that's true, but actually Russian
language has a lot of Tatar words and,

00:15:18.255 --> 00:15:23.370
uh, Uh, uh, Russian language has a lot
of, uh, words from different cultures

00:15:23.520 --> 00:15:29.640
and, uh, now, uh, you know, uh, in Russian
politicians, they're trying to fight, uh,

00:15:30.180 --> 00:15:32.670
against the influence of other languages.

00:15:32.670 --> 00:15:37.470
They want to keep Russian pure, which
is absolutely not possible because,

00:15:37.470 --> 00:15:43.110
uh, Russian language has so many words
with, uh, different roots from different

00:15:43.110 --> 00:15:47.870
language, which even the words you think
they're pure Russian, but they're not.

00:15:49.090 --> 00:15:49.380
Yeah.

00:15:49.530 --> 00:15:50.564
No, and that's true of every.

00:15:51.570 --> 00:15:54.090
You know, every language is
influenced and, and it'll continue.

00:15:54.090 --> 00:15:57.090
You know, I, I'm, right now I'm
learning Persian and Arabic and Turkish

00:15:57.090 --> 00:16:01.410
and there have 15% shared vocabulary
for all kinds of historical reasons.

00:16:02.350 --> 00:16:03.750
Japanese is full of loan words.

00:16:03.750 --> 00:16:06.030
Japanese is 50% Chinese words.

00:16:06.150 --> 00:16:09.300
I mean, every, there is no pure,
there is no pure out there.

00:16:09.305 --> 00:16:11.520
There's no, no, no pure
people, no pure language.

00:16:12.090 --> 00:16:15.584
We live in a, you know, connected world.

00:16:15.885 --> 00:16:17.324
So, but that's interesting.

00:16:17.324 --> 00:16:20.954
So, uh, after a while then living in
Ukraine, people would assume that you're

00:16:20.954 --> 00:16:23.775
Ukrainian if they interacted with you?

00:16:24.015 --> 00:16:28.995
Uh, if I speak Ukrainian, uh, some people
they don't believe I was born in Moscow

00:16:28.995 --> 00:16:33.015
and, uh, I'm from Moscow, from Russia,
so they, they think I'm Ukrainian.

00:16:33.015 --> 00:16:33.285
Yes.

00:16:33.285 --> 00:16:34.515
I, it happened to me

00:16:35.805 --> 00:16:38.114
living in Washington D.C.

00:16:38.875 --> 00:16:42.944
You've, to what extent is there
increased interest in learning Ukrainian.

00:16:43.470 --> 00:16:44.910
Amongst Americans, let's say.

00:16:45.550 --> 00:16:47.415
any at all or not so much?

00:16:47.955 --> 00:16:52.935
I don't know any numbers, but I
definitely see that, uh, a lot of,

00:16:52.935 --> 00:16:58.275
uh, Russian speaking Ukrainians
switch to Ukrainian now, and I

00:16:58.275 --> 00:17:00.075
have a, I have friends like that.

00:17:00.130 --> 00:17:00.740
Mm-hmm.

00:17:00.825 --> 00:17:05.050
Uh, for example, I, uh, have a friend
I recorded an interview with her to,

00:17:05.050 --> 00:17:09.280
for my YouTube channel by the way,
she's from Kharkiv and, uh, she's

00:17:09.285 --> 00:17:11.890
from, uh, Russian speaking family.

00:17:12.010 --> 00:17:12.099
Mm-hmm.

00:17:12.460 --> 00:17:14.560
Uh, she was predominantly
Russian speaking.

00:17:14.620 --> 00:17:17.890
Uh, she knew Ukrainian from
school, but she didn't use it much.

00:17:18.000 --> 00:17:18.440
Right.

00:17:18.444 --> 00:17:21.070
And, uh, when the war started,
and, you know, what happened with

00:17:21.070 --> 00:17:22.420
Kharkiv during the war mm-hmm.

00:17:22.770 --> 00:17:26.650
uh, and she made a decision,
she wrote to everybody.

00:17:26.800 --> 00:17:32.320
I do remember I, um, received a message
from her, uh, saying that, uh, from

00:17:32.320 --> 00:17:34.595
that day I'm switching to Ukrainian.

00:17:34.805 --> 00:17:39.095
Uh, I mean, she switched to Ukrainian
with those people who can speak Ukrainian.

00:17:39.095 --> 00:17:43.985
So she knew I can speak Ukrainian so
she, um, she sent this message to me.

00:17:44.045 --> 00:17:49.264
So a lot of people in the diaspora,
they, uh, they uh, switched to Ukrainian

00:17:49.264 --> 00:17:51.185
now, and I see a lot of movement.

00:17:51.285 --> 00:17:55.275
For example, uh, like, uh,
camps, uh, summer camps for

00:17:55.275 --> 00:17:57.645
kids in Ukrainian and in Kazakh.

00:17:57.645 --> 00:18:02.685
By the way, it was the first year, I
live six years here, and it was first

00:18:02.685 --> 00:18:05.965
time I saw Kazakh camp, uh, this summer.

00:18:06.650 --> 00:18:07.070
Wow.

00:18:07.075 --> 00:18:11.639
And, uh, the same thing in Ukrainian
people start, uh, activities

00:18:11.639 --> 00:18:13.620
for kids in Ukrainian camps.

00:18:13.620 --> 00:18:18.030
Uh, theater, I, by the way, I'm
involved in the new year performance.

00:18:18.030 --> 00:18:23.310
I'm playing, uh, in the Ukrainian, uh,
amateur theater with, uh, for, for the,

00:18:23.310 --> 00:18:25.350
it's a kids performance for the new year.

00:18:26.260 --> 00:18:26.750
Yeah.

00:18:26.754 --> 00:18:26.820
Yeah.

00:18:27.030 --> 00:18:28.080
Oh, that's very interesting.

00:18:28.080 --> 00:18:33.070
You know, and, and, um, Kazakh, I mean,
after all that, it's a huge country

00:18:33.460 --> 00:18:37.980
Kazakhstan, huge country and uh, I
don't know how close it is to Turkish.

00:18:38.505 --> 00:18:40.575
But, uh, maybe we could
close on that theme.

00:18:40.715 --> 00:18:44.865
There, there is no language that's
less important than other languages.

00:18:44.895 --> 00:18:49.875
I, I really believe that, uh, a certain
language may be more important to

00:18:49.880 --> 00:18:53.445
me because I live in a country where
that language is spoken or because

00:18:53.445 --> 00:18:55.125
I have an interest in that culture.

00:18:55.335 --> 00:18:58.995
But, you know, objectively all
languages are equally important.

00:18:59.085 --> 00:19:03.320
It may be, you know, everyone
knows of Shakespeare or of Tolstoy

00:19:03.660 --> 00:19:07.770
because lots of PR behind those
languages, behind those works of art.

00:19:07.860 --> 00:19:11.820
Maybe the works of literature from
Kazakhstan, from Ukraine that are equally

00:19:12.330 --> 00:19:13.560
valuable, but no one has read them.

00:19:13.560 --> 00:19:14.640
No one knows about them.

00:19:14.940 --> 00:19:17.460
And so I think it's very important
to treat each language as

00:19:17.460 --> 00:19:19.020
being, uh, equally important.

00:19:20.179 --> 00:19:20.475
Yes.

00:19:20.655 --> 00:19:24.405
And I also wanted to mention, I, I
think it's very important when we are

00:19:24.405 --> 00:19:29.685
talking about Ukraine and uh, Ukraine,
uh, as a multicultural country mm-hmm.

00:19:29.925 --> 00:19:33.225
uh, I think it's very important
to mention Crimean Tatar language.

00:19:33.465 --> 00:19:33.915
Of course.

00:19:33.915 --> 00:19:38.054
Uh, and, uh, I actually started
learning Crimean Tatar . I stopped,

00:19:38.115 --> 00:19:41.985
uh, because it was difficult and
they didn't have time, and I didn't

00:19:41.985 --> 00:19:43.814
have that motivation, uh Right.

00:19:43.814 --> 00:19:46.505
But I want to get uh, to that language.

00:19:46.565 --> 00:19:48.455
Uh, one day someday.

00:19:48.665 --> 00:19:51.955
I have a friend, a very, very
good friend, Crimean Tatar.

00:19:52.085 --> 00:19:55.565
Uh, she, uh, uh, she's from
Ukraine and we, uh, at some

00:19:55.565 --> 00:19:57.035
point we lived, uh, together.

00:19:57.245 --> 00:20:02.165
And, uh, she's Crimean Tatar and she's
a teacher of Crimean Tatar  language.

00:20:02.435 --> 00:20:07.435
Uh, and, uh, she was trying to teach
me, I even, uh, still have that notebook

00:20:07.440 --> 00:20:09.485
with my notes and Crimean Tatar.

00:20:09.965 --> 00:20:10.835
Uh, and, uh, I.

00:20:11.600 --> 00:20:15.330
Um, it's a very important, um,
it's very important as a sign of

00:20:15.330 --> 00:20:17.220
support of this, uh, population.

00:20:17.280 --> 00:20:17.370
Yes.

00:20:17.460 --> 00:20:20.370
Because they were always
oppressed by the Russian Empire

00:20:20.375 --> 00:20:22.050
and then by Soviet Union and...

00:20:22.230 --> 00:20:26.370
and now Crimean Tatar  oppressed
in the, in the next Crimea.

00:20:26.375 --> 00:20:29.340
So, uh, I think it's important
to talk about their language

00:20:29.340 --> 00:20:30.590
and their culture as well.

00:20:30.870 --> 00:20:31.530
Absolutely.

00:20:31.860 --> 00:20:32.490
Okay.

00:20:32.610 --> 00:20:35.160
Well, listen, I think I have the
impression we could go on forever

00:20:35.160 --> 00:20:38.880
